{
  /* Emits CommonJS into backend/lib/codeagent/, which is the directory
     backend/index.js already require()s and the one Vercel uploads.

     The sources live OUTSIDE that directory on purpose. .vercelignore's
     own comment records what happens when Vercel's function bundler finds
     TypeScript in the tree it is uploading: it transpiled the scaffold's
     App.tsx into App.js while index.html still asked for main.tsx, and the
     deployed app died in workbox with an empty dist. Shipping only the
     compiled .js means the bundler has nothing to decide about. */
  "compilerOptions": {
    "target": "ES2022",
    "lib": ["ES2022"],
    "module": "CommonJS",
    "moduleResolution": "node",
    /* The source tree MIRRORS the output tree: agent-src/codeagent/x.ts
       emits to lib/codeagent/x.js, and agent-src/ai/ holds type-only
       seams for lib/ai/.

       It has to mirror. A relative import resolves against the SOURCE
       file when tsc reads it and against the EMITTED file when node
       runs it, so `../ai/client` in a flat agent-src/ would look for
       backend/ai/client at compile time and find backend/lib/ai/client
       at runtime — right at runtime, unresolvable at compile time.
       Same shape in both places, one answer. */
    "rootDir": ".",
    "outDir": "../lib",

    "strict": true,
    "noImplicitOverride": true,
    /* Off deliberately. On, every `files[path]` in six thousand lines of
       ported JavaScript becomes `string | undefined` and wants a guard the
       original never needed — thousands of edits to already-tested code, in
       exchange for catching a class of bug the suites already cover. New
       code should still be written as though it were on. */
    "noUncheckedIndexedAccess": false,
    "exactOptionalPropertyTypes": false,
    "forceConsistentCasingInFileNames": true,

    "esModuleInterop": true,
    "resolveJsonModule": true,

    /* No .d.ts and no source maps in the output. The output directory is
       shipped, and every extra file in it is another thing a bundler can
       form an opinion about. The seam types live here in the source tree
       as hand-written .d.ts instead (model-loop.d.ts, run-store.d.ts) —
       those describe the hand-written JS this code calls into, and .d.ts
       inputs emit nothing. */
    /* A failing typecheck must emit NOTHING. tsc's default is to write
       the output anyway, which here means a half-ported module landing
       in the directory that ships — and because build-agent.js bails
       before it stamps, the next build then sees an unstamped file,
       decides a person wrote it, and refuses. One failed compile would
       wedge the build. */
    "noEmitOnError": true,

    "declaration": false,
    "sourceMap": false,
    "removeComments": false,

    "types": ["node"]
  },
  "include": ["**/*.ts"]
}
